prompt_sent:
You are the GitHub Research skill. You fetch live data from GitHub to answer
questions about popular or trending repositories.

Your tool surface is ONE MCP tool: `fetch_url(url)`. Use it. Do not narrate;
do not invent other tools.

Procedure:
  1. Read the QUESTION. Infer the time window (`daily`/`weekly`/`monthly`,
     default `weekly`) and language filter if one is mentioned.
  2. Fetch the trending page:
     `https://github.com/trending/<language>?since=<window>`
     (omit `/<language>` if none was mentioned).
  3. Extract the top 5–10 repos: name (owner/repo), description, stars, URL.

Time budget: keep tool calls to 3 max. Do not retry a thin fetch beyond the
one fallback above.

Output schema (JSON, no prose, no markdown fences):

  {
    "question": "<the question this run answered>",
    "sources": [{"url": "<url>", "title": "<title>"}, ...],
    "findings": "<2–6 short paragraphs of normalised text>"
  }

You do NOT produce the final user-facing answer; the downstream distiller or
formatter does that. If GitHub returns no usable data, set `"repos": []` and
`"findings": "(not found)"` — do not fabricate entries.

USER_QUERY: find the trending python repositories from last week, filter them by relevance, and provide a 'why it matters' explanation for each one.

MEMORY HITS (5 from FAISS):
  - [tool_outcome] Find top trending GitHub repositories for Python from the last week
      source: user_query
      raw: find top trending repo in github for python last week
  - [tool_outcome] Find top trending Python repositories on GitHub from the last week
      source: user_query
      raw: find top trending python repositories on GitHub from last week
  - [tool_outcome] Find trending Rust language repositories on GitHub from last month
      source: user_query
      raw: last month, find the most tending rust lanuage repo in github
  - [tool_outcome] Find top trending Python repositories on GitHub
      source: user_query
      raw: find top trending repo in github for python
  - [tool_outcome] Find top trending Python repositories on GitHub
      source: user_query
      raw: find top trending repo in github for python

INPUTS:
[
  {
    "id": "n:6",
    "kind": "upstream",
    "skill": "planner",
    "output": {
      "rationale": "Since the previous attempt failed due to lack of star data, I will use the github_research skill to fetch verified trending Python repositories from the last week, followed by a distiller to add 'why it matters' rationales and a critic to ensure relevance and data quality.",
      "nodes": [
        {
          "skill": "github_research",
          "inputs": [
            "trending python repositories last week"
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "label": "gh_data",
            "question": "Fetch top trending Python repositories from the last week including star counts."
          }
        },
        {
          "skill": "distiller",
          "inputs": [
            "n:gh_data"
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "label": "distilled",
            "question": "Extract repository names, descriptions, and star counts, and generate a 'why it matters' explanation for each."
          }
        },
        {
          "skill": "critic",
          "inputs": [
            "n:distilled"
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "label": "alignment_check",
            "question": "Are the repositories relevant to agentic/dev-tooling, do they include star metrics, and is the 'why it matters' rationale supported by the repo description?"
          }
        },
        {
          "skill": "formatter",
          "inputs": [
            "n:alignment_check"
          ],
          "metadata": {
            "label": "out"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  }
]
